# The Old Woman in the Mirror

**Series:** Campfire Horror Tales

**Setting:** Saint-Clair-des-Bois, Québec

**Genre:** Atmospheric Horror

**Status:** Draft 1 (Spoken Revision)

---

## The Old Woman in the Mirror

Susie was a very special little girl. She grew up just the other side of the lake, up on the hill where the horses now live, with the old man who chops the wood.

She was born way back in 1902, back when these woods were nearly uninhabited. She was one of about twelve children born to a family that managed to escape Germany and flee to Canada. It was a war bride scenario. They arrived by horse and carriage, dropped off in the wilderness to choose a slice of paradise or hell, and build a home and start a family.

Well, that they did.

Pop went out to the woods each fall to trap various wild animals for fur, living in the deathly cold all winter out in the wilderness with other men from the small cabin community.

Occasionally, they wouldn't make it back. They would die from sickness or cold, and it would often be too difficult to carry their bodies back. Their bones would be left out there to rot beneath the trees, or beneath the ice at the bottom of one of the surrounding lakes.

If they returned in May, there was almost always a new baby waiting. Mama was always giving birth at home, alone and screaming. There was no medical care, but if they were lucky, some of the other women from the small cabin community would help. Sometimes even the older children had to help.

And some mothers wouldn't make it. They would bleed to death in the very bed they shared with the baby born just the year before.

Those who survived the harsh Canadian woods would have to drag Mama out to the freezing shed so she could be buried in the spring.

These women were weathered beyond their years. Skinny and calloused, their skin leathered and cracked, wiry muscles hardened from years of heavy labour.

Older children watched over the young ones, giving their parents the time they needed to prepare enough food and supplies to keep the family alive.

But even the children weren't safe, often dying young from sickness or some unfortunate accident. They are buried with the dead up on the hill, their small bones making up much of the cemetery behind the little chapel on the road.

But our story is of little Susie, who grew up in one such small cabin in the woods.

She was the oldest daughter.

Her mama was tough and hard, surviving all twelve births.

Her papa was stern and proud. He would often brag about his winter trapping, sometimes fortunate enough to find an animal still alive, withering in pain in one of his traps, only to retrieve it and skin it alive, savouring the warm blood as a soothing and invigorating tea.

Susie shuddered at the thought of being married off to such a mirrored life.

Baby after baby.

Born.

Screamed into the night.

Only to watch some of them die without simple medicine.

To wonder if her husband would come home alive...

...or lay rotting somewhere in the woods.

She had little in the way of toys. No books. No dolls.

But as the oldest girl, her mother had gifted her a mirror.

A looking glass.

It had been brought all the way from Germany.

It had been handed down to each of the oldest daughters in the family.

It was not just any looking glass.

Well...

It was to everyone else.

But not to Susie.

From a very young age she had visitors that appeared through the looking glass.

Old ancestors would appear.

Some young and beautiful.

Others old and decrepit.

Some so horrifying she would flee, run from the reflection, and hide the looking glass beneath the blankets of her shared mattress.

But she would always come back.

Curiosity always got the better of Susie.

It became her only game.

Her only entertainment.

Her imagination.

She could spend hours staring into it.

Some of the faces were kind.

They would sing songs.

Tell stories.

But others...

Others would scream.

Eyes empty sockets.

Blood dripping from the corners of jagged, tooth-filled grins.

The day came when her mama sat her down.

She was to be married.

She was sixteen after all.

A gift, of sorts, to a neighbouring boy known for torturing frogs and beating smaller children.

His name was Frank.

It was surely no gift to Susie.

She ran again and hid beneath the covers of her still-shared mattress.

She cried for what felt like days.

Finally she reached into her little treasure box.

She lifted out her special mirror.

For the first time...

She saw herself.

She gasped.

Why...

It was her own reflection.

Had she lost the magic?

But then...

Slowly...

The face began to change.

It became her as a little girl.

Then a pre-teen.

Then herself again.

The faces were crying.

Tears of blood ran down their cheeks.

She touched her own face.

It was dry.

Then the reflections changed again.

Bloodied.

Broken nose.

Missing teeth.

Black eyes.

The horrors of her future face were too much to bear.

But still...

She stared.

The images shifted once more.

She saw herself in old age.

Toothless.

Withered.

Empty eyes.

Hollow.

She stared in horror at the faces of her own life.

"No...

Please...

No..."

Just then a familiar face appeared.

Not her own.

But that of a kindly old woman.

"You can escape," it whispered.

"How?" she cried.

"How?"

The old woman smiled softly.

"Join us.

You only have to ask."

"Please...

Anything...

Just let me escape this horrible life with Frank.

Please...

Let me join you."

The old woman's grin spread slowly across her face.

Her eyes twinkled.

Hungry.

Her mouth watered.

The looking glass began to glow.

Susie stood frozen.

Unable to move.

Unable to scream.

Unable even to blink.

Then...

Her soul was pulled into the mirror.

Her body vanished.

And just like that...

She was gone.

Susie was never seen again.

Hours later her mother came looking for her.

There was no trace.

Enraged that her daughter had fled her wedding just as old Aunt Hilda had done years before, she grabbed Susie's cherished looking glass and hurled it into the lake.

"Cursed thing!" she screamed.

"Just like old Aunt Hilda!

Running from your troubles!"

The mirror slowly sank beneath the clear water.

As it disappeared...

Susie's face looked back.

Screaming.

"No!

Help me!"

But it was too late.

That was many years ago.

Yet those who peer into the clear waters beside the old dock—if they have the gift—still see the reflections of tortured souls.

Susie.

Hilda.

And all those who begged for a better life before them.

Those who chose...

...the other side of the looking glass.

---

**So be careful what you wish for...**

**...for you might just get it.**